# Byte-exact context-layer dedupe (issue #225; #155 Phase 3).
#
# `zirv ctx compile` skips injecting the canonical `.zirv/context/*.md` layer
# only when the generated native file (CLAUDE.md / AGENTS.md) byte-exactly
# matches what `render_generated` (src/commands/ctx/context_cli.rs) would emit
# right now from the on-disk sources. That render embeds the sources' line
# endings verbatim plus hardcoded LF separators, so the comparison survives a
# checkout only if BOTH sides are byte-stable across platforms:
#
# - `/.zirv/context/*.md text eol=lf` pins the render inputs to LF on every
#   checkout, regardless of core.autocrlf, making the render output uniform LF
#   everywhere.
# - `/CLAUDE.md -text` / `/AGENTS.md -text` freeze the committed render output
#   exactly as generated (no normalization on either add or checkout).
#
# Patterns are anchored to the repo root: an unanchored `CLAUDE.md` matches at
# any depth, and on case-insensitive filesystems it even captures
# `.zirv/context/claude.md`.
/CLAUDE.md -text
/AGENTS.md -text
/.zirv/context/*.md text eol=lf

# Issue #326: the `zirv ctx run --compact` output fixtures are executed by
# `sh`/`cmd` verbatim, so their line endings are load-bearing (`git archive`
# would otherwise hand a CRLF `.sh` to `sh`, which chokes on it).
/tests/fixtures/noisy-output.sh text eol=lf
/tests/fixtures/noisy-output.cmd text eol=crlf

# Issues #353 and #470: both wire-fixture sets are compared BYTE FOR BYTE
# against what `serde_json::to_string_pretty` produces (LF), so a CRLF checkout
# on Windows fails the compatibility guard for a reason that has nothing to do
# with the wire. `runtime/v1` (#470) shipped without this pin and its three
# round-trip tests fail on any Windows checkout with core.autocrlf on.
/tests/fixtures/protocol/v1/*.json text eol=lf
/tests/fixtures/runtime/v1/*.json text eol=lf
